item.description:
arXiv:2610.03982v1 Announce Type: new Abstract: We study constant-delay enumeration for conjunctive queries with negation ($	exttt{CQ}^{
eg}$). Prior work defined \emph{signed-acyclicity}, which characterizes the class of queries where linear preprocessing time is achievable, but little has been known beyond this. We introduce a new hypergraph width measure for $	exttt{CQ}^{
eg}$, the \emph{signed fractional hypertree width} ($	extsf{sfhw}$), defined by requiring that a single variable order simultaneously handle every subset of the negative atoms. We show that $	extsf{sfhw}$ strictly generalizes signed-acyclicity (recovered when $	extsf{sfhw} = 1$) and fractional hypertree width (recovered on queries without negation). Our main algorithmic result is a variable-elimination algorithm that achieves constant-delay enumeration on input $I$ for any full $	exttt{CQ}^{
eg}$ query with preprocessing time $O(|I|^{	extsf{sfhw}})$. We further show that using multiple variable orders can improve this bound, exhibiting an $O(|I|^{3/2})$ algorithm for $k$-cycle queries with at least one negative edge.
